    description: "Sealed grow rooms need dedicated dehumidification sized to transpiration loads. Learn how vertical farm HVAC handles latent load, VPD, and reheat.",
    h1: "What Are Indoor Farm HVAC and Dehumidification Requirements?",
    answer: "The defining HVAC problem in a vertical farm is latent load. A healthy canopy transpires nearly all the water it is irrigated with, which means a sealed grow room accumulates moisture at a rate comfort-cooling equipment was never designed to remove. Sizing starts from the crop water budget: leafy greens commonly transpire the equivalent of 0.5 to 1.5 pints per square foot of canopy per day, and the dehumidification plant must remove that moisture continuously while holding the room at its temperature and vapor pressure deficit setpoints.\n\nStandard packaged rooftop units fail here for a specific reason: they couple sensible and latent capacity. When the thermostat is satisfied, the compressor cycles off and dehumidification stops, so humidity climbs exactly when the crop is transpiring hardest. CEA facilities need equipment that decouples the two, either dedicated dehumidification units with hot-gas or condenser reheat, or desiccant systems where the climate and utility rates justify them. The reheat piece is non-negotiable; without it, the room overcools every time the system wrings moisture out of the air.\n\nAir distribution deserves as much design attention as equipment selection. Multi-tier racks create stratified microclimates, with the top tier running warm and dry while the bottom tier sits cool and saturated. The mechanical design has to deliver conditioned air to every tier, which usually means horizontal airflow strategies, perforated distribution, or under-rack supply coordinated directly with the racking layout. Computational fluid dynamics modeling pays for itself on large rooms by revealing dead zones before they become disease zones.\n\nControls tie it all together. The sequence of operation should control to vapor pressure deficit rather than relative humidity alone, because VPD is what the plant actually experiences, and it should stage dehumidification, cooling, and reheat against independent temperature and humidity sensors at multiple rack heights. Trend logging of those sensors is what lets the grower correlate environmental excursions with crop issues weeks later.",
