        heading: "Sizing dehumidification from transpiration",
        body: "Start with the irrigation schedule, not a rule of thumb. The mechanical engineer needs the grower's daily water use per square foot of canopy at peak crop stage, then applies a transpiration fraction, typically 85 to 95 percent for dense leafy canopies, to get the latent load in pints or pounds per day. That number gets checked against the lighting schedule, because transpiration peaks under full PPFD and drops in dark periods, which is why the system needs turndown, not just peak capacity.\n\nConvert the moisture load to equipment capacity using the unit's rated moisture removal at the actual entering air conditions, not the catalog rating point. Dehumidifier performance falls off as room temperature drops, so a unit rated at 80°F and 60 percent RH delivers meaningfully less in a 68°F lettuce room. The engineer derates every selection to the real operating point and documents the basis so the grower understands the margin.\n\nBuild in redundancy the way the crop demands it. A single dehumidification unit serving a sealed room is a single point of failure for the entire crop cycle; N+1 configuration or split systems across independently controlled zones keeps one failure from becoming a total loss. For high-value crops, that redundancy is cheap insurance against a humidity spike that invites powdery mildew or botrytis overnight.",
