    description: "Vertical farm lighting runs 30-60 W per sq ft of canopy. Learn DLI targets, LED efficacy, service sizing, and lighting controls for CEA facilities.",
    h1: "What Are Greenhouse Supplemental Lighting Electrical Loads?",
    answer: "Lighting dominates vertical farm electrical design the way process equipment dominates a factory. The engineering starts from the crop's daily light integral target: leafy greens need roughly 12 to 17 moles per square meter per day, fruiting crops like tomatoes and strawberries need 20 to 30, and microgreens get by on 6 to 12. Divide the DLI by the photoperiod to get the required photosynthetic photon flux density, then divide by fixture efficacy to get watts per square foot of canopy.\n\nWith modern LED fixtures delivering 2.5 to 3.5 micromoles per joule, a lettuce canopy at 250 micromoles per square meter per second lands around 30 to 40 watts per square foot, while a strawberry canopy at higher PPFD pushes 50 to 60. Multiply across the canopy area and the numbers get serious fast: 10,000 square feet of canopy at 40 watts per square foot is 400 kilowatts of lighting load before HVAC, dehumidification, pumps, or controls. That single load routinely exceeds the entire existing electrical service of a retrofit warehouse.\n\nService sizing has to account for the whole picture. Lighting at full photoperiod coincides with peak cooling and dehumidification, so the demand calculation stacks lighting, mechanical, and process loads with appropriate demand factors rather than assuming diversity that does not exist. The engineer also evaluates power quality: hundreds of LED drivers can introduce harmonics that overheat neutrals and distort voltage, which means harmonic analysis and possibly oversized neutrals or harmonic-rated transformers belong in the design.\n\nUtility coordination starts early because it has the longest lead time on the project. A load letter documenting the new service size goes to the utility during schematic design, and the engineer designs the lighting layout with an eye on what service the utility can actually deliver and when. Value-engineering the DLI targets or photoperiod against the available service is a legitimate design conversation, and it is far cheaper in month two than in month eight.",
