        body: "The calculation chain runs DLI to PPFD to fixture count to watts to panels, and every link needs documentation. The horticultural consultant or grower sets the DLI per crop and growth stage; the lighting designer converts that to average PPFD over the photoperiod and selects fixtures with published PPF and efficacy; the electrical engineer lays out the fixtures, calculates branch circuit loads, and builds the panel schedules with spare capacity for the controls and sensor networks every modern grow room carries.\n\nLighting controls are an energy code compliance item, not an accessory. Most energy codes now require multilevel or continuous dimming, occupancy or vacancy shutoff in non-grow areas, and scheduled shutoff, and grow lighting interfaces with the building automation system for photoperiod control. The design documents the lighting power density calculations and control sequences the energy compliance forms require, which keeps plan check moving.\n\nEmergency and egress lighting needs separate attention in facilities where grow lighting shuts off on schedule. When 400 kilowatts of LED goes dark at the end of photoperiod, workers still need code-compliant egress illumination, and the design provides it on circuits independent of the grow lighting controls.",
