        body: "Most vertical farm projects need a new or upgraded utility service, and the utility's timeline governs the project more than any other single factor. The load letter goes out in schematic design with the connected load, demand load, voltage, and phasing the engineer has calculated, and the design keeps a dialog open as lighting selections firm up. If the utility quotes a nine-month lead time for a new transformer, the project schedule absorbs that reality in month two instead of discovering it in month eight.\n\nGenerator backup for lighting is a business decision the engineer helps quantify. Full lighting backup for a large canopy needs a generator in the megawatt class; partial backup that keeps photoperiod-critical zones lit while shedding the rest needs careful load-shed sequencing. Either way, the transfer scheme, selective coordination of the protective devices, and the control interface between the generator and the lighting control system belong in the construction documents.\n\nDemand charges often dominate the utility bill more than energy charges do. Since grow lighting runs at full output for 12 to 18 hours daily, the facility's peak demand is essentially its lighting load plus coincident mechanical, and the electrical design can help by staggering zone start times to shave the morning ramp. That staggering gets written into the lighting control sequence, not left as an operating suggestion.",
