    slug: "vertical-farming-energy-modeling",
    title: "Vertical Farm Energy Modeling and Utility Costs?",
    description: "Lighting is 60-70% of vertical farm energy use. Learn how energy modeling sizes services, predicts utility costs, and finds efficiency opportunities.",
    h1: "How Is Energy Use Modeled for a Vertical Farm?",
    answer: "Energy modeling for a vertical farm starts where the loads are: the lighting schedule. Every grow zone's photoperiod, fixture wattage, and dimming profile go into the model as hourly inputs, because lighting at 30 to 60 watts per square foot of canopy running 12 to 18 hours a day is the dominant energy end use, commonly 60 to 70 percent of the facility total. The model stacks the mechanical systems on top, with cooling and dehumidification loads derived from the lighting heat, the transpiration latent load, and the envelope, all varying hour by hour against local weather data.\n\nThe output the owner actually needs is the utility bill, not just kilowatt-hours. Demand charges, time-of-use rates, and ratchet clauses can make the monthly peak more expensive than the total energy, and a vertical farm's load profile, flat and high during photoperiod, interacts with rate structures in ways a standard commercial model misses. The engineer runs the model against the actual utility tariff, tests scenarios like staggered zone start times to shave the morning demand ramp, and quantifies what each efficiency measure saves in dollars, not just energy.\n\nEfficiency opportunities get evaluated honestly against crop impact. Higher-efficacy LED fixtures cut both lighting energy and the cooling load from fixture heat, which is why lighting upgrades cascade through the whole model. Waste heat recovery from dehumidification reheat, condensing heat recovery for water heating, and economizer operation in suitable climates all show up as line items with payback periods. What the model does not do is trade crop performance for energy savings without the grower's explicit decision; a DLI reduction that saves 10 percent on energy but costs 15 percent of yield is a bad trade the model makes visible.\n\nThe model also sizes infrastructure. Peak coincident demand sets the utility service size, the generator size if backup is planned, and the capacity of the main switchgear. Getting this number right during design prevents the two expensive failures: an undersized service that constrains the operation, and an oversized one that paid for capacity the facility never uses. The model documents its assumptions so future expansions can rerun it instead of starting over.",
    directAnswer: "A vertical farm energy model combines lighting load from DLI targets, HVAC and dehumidification from psychrometric analysis, and process loads into an hourly simulation. Lighting typically represents 60 to 70 percent of total energy, and the model predicts both consumption and demand charges.",
