        body: "The model's peak demand number becomes the load letter to the utility, and its credibility matters. A load letter backed by an hourly simulation with documented assumptions gets a faster, more accurate utility response than a round-number estimate, because the utility's planners can see the load shape, not just the peak. Power factor, harmonic profile, and motor starting inrush ride along in the coordination package.\n\nRate selection is a design decision. The engineer compares the modeled load profile against available tariffs, general service versus time-of-use versus economic development rates, and recommends the rate that minimizes the modeled annual cost. Some utilities offer agricultural or controlled-environment rates with favorable demand structures; identifying and qualifying for them during design is worth real money every month for the life of the facility.\n\nThe model stays alive after construction. Metered data from the building automation system gets compared against the model quarterly in the first year, and variances get investigated: a zone using 20 percent more energy than modeled has a story, whether it is a failed damper, an overridden schedule, or a crop plan change. That feedback loop is what turns an energy model from a permit document into an operating tool.",
