        body: "Detection technology gets selected for the environment. High-humidity grow rooms can nuisance-alarm ionization or photoelectric spot detectors, so the design may use aspirating smoke detection with sampling ports positioned for the rack airflow patterns, or heat detection where smoke detection is impractical. The engineer documents the selection rationale so the plan reviewer and the alarm contractor share the same expectations.\n\nCO2 enrichment introduces a gas hazard the life-safety design must address. CO2 monitors with audible and visual alarms at entrances, ventilation interlocks that purge the space on high concentration, and signage at every enriched room are standard provisions. The mechanical and fire alarm designs coordinate the interlock sequences, and the operations manual trains workers on the alarm response because CO2 is odorless and the hazard is invisible.\n\nFertilizer and chemical storage brings the fire code's hazardous materials provisions into play. Concentrated acids, sanitizers, and some nutrient formulations have storage quantity thresholds, separation requirements, and secondary containment rules that the architectural and fire protection designs address together. The hazardous materials inventory and the code analysis in the permit set show the reviewer exactly how each material is stored and why the arrangement complies.",
