    description: "Vertical farms face occupancy classification, high-piled storage, and energy code questions. Learn how CEA facilities navigate code and plan check.",
    h1: "How Do Building Codes Apply to Vertical Farms?",
    answer: "The building code never contemplated vertical farming, so the first engineering task on any CEA project is a code analysis that maps the facility's actual operations onto the code's occupancy categories. Most indoor farms land in factory or storage occupancy depending on the balance of growing, processing, and packing, with mercantile or business occupancy for retail and office components. Greenhouses sometimes qualify for agricultural exemptions that vanish the moment significant processing, packing, or public access enters the program, and the analysis documents exactly where each space falls.\n\nOccupancy classification cascades into everything: allowable area and height, construction type, fire separation between the grow areas and any retail or office, plumbing fixture counts, and accessibility requirements. A facility that grows, packs, and sells on one site can easily contain three occupancies with rated separations between them, and the life-safety plans show those separations with the door, damper, and penetration details that make them real. Discovering the occupancy mix during plan check instead of during design is how schedules slip by months.\n\nThe energy code brings its own CEA-specific questions. Horticultural lighting is generally exempt from standard lighting power density limits, but the exemption must be documented and the rest of the building still complies, including lighting controls and envelope requirements. Mechanical system efficiency requirements apply to the HVAC plant, and the compliance forms need an engineer who has prepared them for process-heavy facilities before, because the standard compliance software assumes office buildings.\n\nPlumbing and health department coordination runs in parallel with building permit. Food production triggers health department plan review in most jurisdictions, with its own requirements for finishes, plumbing, and equipment that overlay the building code. The permit strategy sequences these reviews, building, health, fire, and sometimes agricultural departments, so one agency's corrections do not invalidate another's approval, and the submittal package tells a consistent story to all of them.",
